= Solution
{parent=5}

For closed connected oriented $d$-manifolds, the <degree of a map between oriented manifolds> is the integer defined by $f_*[M]=(\deg f)[N]$ in $H_d(N;\mathbb Z)$. If $y\in N$ has finite fiber $\{x_1,\ldots,x_r\}$, choose disjoint small neighbourhoods $U_i$ containing no other points of that fiber. The map of local <relative homology> groups
$$
H_d(U_i,U_i\setminus\{x_i\};\mathbb Z)\longrightarrow H_d(N,N\setminus\{y\};\mathbb Z)
$$
sends the local orientation generator at $x_i$ to $d_i$ times the one at $y$. This integer is its <local degree of a continuous map>, independent of the small neighbourhood by <excision>. Excision also identifies $H_d(M,M\setminus f^{-1}(y))$ with the direct sum of the local groups, and the global fundamental class restricts to their orientation generators. Naturality of the maps to relative homology therefore proves the <degree as a sum of local degrees>:
$$
\boxed{\deg f=\sum_{i=1}^r\deg_{x_i}f.}
$$
The empty fiber gives degree zero. For smooth maps at a regular value, a local degree is the sign of the derivative determinant, but general continuous maps can have larger local multiplicities.

= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Using complex coordinates near zero, $F_k(z)=z^k$ for $k>0$ induces winding number $k$ on a small circle, so its local relative homology multiplier is $k$. For $k<0$, $F_k(z)=\overline z^{\,|k|}$ has local degree $k$, since conjugation reverses the winding. For zero, take $F_0(z)=|z|^2\in\mathbb C$: its zero fiber is the single point zero, while the image of a small circle is a single positive real point, so its local degree is zero. Equivalently a nearby nonreal target has no preimages.

Each of these maps is proper and hence extends continuously over the one-point compactifications to a map $S^2\to S^2$ with zero as an isolated preimage of zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{\deg_0F_k=k\quad\text{for every }k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
These are <local degrees of arbitrary integer value>. Taking $F_k$ times the identity on $\mathbb R^{d-2}$ gives the same local degrees for every $d\geq2$. In dimension one, an isolated local degree is restricted to $-1,0,1$, so the all-integers assertion is realized in dimensions at least two.

= ii
{parent=5}
